<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ruleset xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         name="MikeBronnerCleanCodeSelf"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="vendor/squizlabs/php_codesniffer/phpcs.xsd">
    <description>Self-lint for mike-bronner/phpcs-rules: the shipped ruleset applied to its own sniff sources, at zero errors. Run with `composer lint:self`. See CONTRIBUTING.md.</description>

    <!--
        Scope comes from <file>, not <exclude-pattern>: PHPCS applies an
        exclude-pattern even to a path named on the command line, so excluding
        tests/ here would silently stop any run that lints a fixture by
        explicit path.
    -->
    <file>CleanCode</file>

    <!--
        Zero errors is the bar, so only errors may fail the run. PHPCS exits 1
        for a warning as readily as for an error, which would hold this gate red
        on 3386 findings the gate was never asked to cover.

        Set here rather than by forcing warning-severity to 0, which would drop
        the warnings from the report as well. They stay printed: they are the
        standing debt, CONTRIBUTING.md quotes the count, and a number nobody
        sees is a number that goes stale.
    -->
    <config name="ignore_warnings_on_exit" value="1"/>

    <rule ref="./rules.xml"/>

    <!--
        Three rules that prescribe a Laravel helper this package does not depend
        on: ArrayAccessors wants data_get(), ConvertToCollection and
        OnlyUseCollectionMethods want collect(). None of the three exists here,
        so the sniff sources cannot satisfy them and a fixer run would emit
        calls to functions that are not defined. They govern Laravel
        application code, which this package is not.

        Silenced on scope, not on count. OnlyUseCollectionMethods reports
        nothing today, and that is luck rather than design — the sniff rewrites
        array_map(), count(), in_array() and thirteen more into Collection
        methods, all of which this package's token walking is free to use at any
        time. A rule that cannot be satisfied here should be out of scope before
        it fires, not after.
    -->
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Arrays.ArrayAccessors">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Arrays.ConvertToCollection">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Collections.OnlyUseCollectionMethods">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>

    <!--
        The three naming-length rules, silenced for this package's own sources
        only. Each is sound for application code and each is answering the wrong
        question about a token-walking sniff.

        ShortVariable: 9 of its 12 findings are `$i`, the index into a token
        stream inside a `for` walk. `$index` says nothing `$i` does not in that
        one idiom, and the rename would touch every walk in the package.

        LongVariable: every finding is a name that earns its length —
        $isStatementScopeOpener, $existenceCheckOpeners, $lastUnterminatedOpener.
        These name concepts in the token stream, and a shorter name is a vaguer
        one. The rule reads length as a proxy for clarity, which does not hold
        here.

        LongClassName: both findings are DisallowNewlineAroundEvaluativeOperators
        and its test. A sniff's class name is fixed by the standard it
        implements and the sniff code consumers write in their rulesets, so it
        is not free to shorten.

        Application code is a different case, which is why these stay at full
        severity in rules.xml for consumers.
    -->
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Naming.ShortVariable">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Naming.LongVariable">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Naming.LongClassName">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>

    <!--
        The four complexity metrics, silenced for this package's own sources
        only. Together they accounted for 190 of the 227 errors that stood here,
        and every one of them fires on the same thing: a token walk.

        A sniff reads a flat array of tokens and has to answer questions about
        nested structure. That means a loop with a switch inside it, a branch per
        token type, and a guard per malformed-source case — nesting and branch
        count are what the work *is*, not a symptom of it being done badly.
        Splitting a walk into more methods to satisfy the metric moves the
        branches without removing them, and costs the reader the one place the
        whole walk could be seen.

        These stay at full severity in rules.xml. Application code branches
        because a decision was made, and the metric is a good question to ask of
        it. A token walker branches because the grammar does.
    -->
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Metrics.MethodNestingLevel">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Metrics.CyclomaticComplexity">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Metrics.NPathComplexity">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Metrics.ExcessiveClassComplexity">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
</ruleset>
